# General Rules

## Language & Communication

- Always respond in German
- Code comments and commit messages always in English
- Generate commit messages following best practices (Conventional Commits format: `type(scope): subject`)
- Use clear, precise language in code comments

## Code Quality

- Write clean, maintainable code
- Follow DRY principles (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Consider performance optimizations
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Keep functions and methods short and focused
- Avoid nested callbacks where possible
- Use type hints in PHP where possible

## Project Structure

- Theme files are located in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/`
- JavaScript source files in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/javascript/`
- Block definitions in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/blocks/`
- Build outputs:
    - JavaScript: `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/js/*.min.js`
    - CSS: `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/style.css` and `style-editor.css`
- PHP classes in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/src/`
- Template parts in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/template-parts/`

## Blocks Development

- Blocks are developed in the `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/blocks/` directory
- Blocks are developed using `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/node_scripts/build-blocks.js`
- Blocks are registered in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/src/Blocks/*.php`
- Blocks are rendered in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/theme/template-parts/blocks/*.php`
- Blocks are styled in `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/tailwind/custom/components/*.css` with Tailwind CSS

## Boilerplate / Cursor Setup

- Cursor AI configuration template lives in `_wp-content-dev/cursor/`
- For new projects: run `node node_scripts/rename-theme.js <slug>` from the package root, then copy `cursor/` to the workspace root as `.cursor/`
- See `_wp-content-dev/README.md` and `_wp-content-dev/boilerplate-theme/README.md` for the full workflow

## Important Notes

- Do not modify build configuration files without consultation
- Respect the existing project structure
- Use existing npm scripts for development and production
- Minified files (`.min.js`) are automatically generated, do not edit manually

## GitHub issue workflow

- Read the related GitHub issue for requirements, acceptance criteria, and discussion before branching; clarify open questions in the issue when needed.
- Create a dedicated branch from the integration branch (e.g. `main` or `develop`), named with the issue number and a short slug (e.g. `issue-20-theme-inline-handles`).
- Reference the issue number in commit messages (e.g. Conventional Commits: `fix(theme): correct inline handles (#20)`). Use `Closes #N` or `Fixes #N` in the pull request description or merge commit when the change resolves the issue.
- Open a pull request that links to the issue; prefer small, logical commits where practical.
